WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.940
It's great to welcome back to the program,
Michael Moss, New York Times best selling

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.130
author, wrote the book Salt, Sugar, Fat.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:15.910
New book out today is Hooked Food Free Will
and How the Food Giants Exploit Our Addictions.

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:17.599
So great to have you back, Michael.

00:00:17.599 --> 00:00:20.070
Hey, nice to see you again, David.

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:26.369
So in the time since we last spoke, we've
learned a lot more about the sort of physically

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:29.409
addictive nature of certain food elements.

00:00:29.409 --> 00:00:31.130
We've learned about sugar.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:36.800
We've we've learned about other elements,
but we've also learned a lot about food marketing,

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.390
nutritionism and so many other aspects that
that operate on a more sort of psychological

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:42.970
rather than physical level.

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:47.030
Talk a little bit about sort of your general
approach to this book and what you're talking

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:50.510
about when you talk about addiction to food.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:55.730
Yeah, so, I mean, I wrote the book and I started
researching it because right up the salt,

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:59.570
sugar, fat came out at the end of it, I was
trying to be a little optimistic and say,

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.490
look, ultimately we're the ones who decide
what to eat and how much.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:08.469
In the very first question I got was, but
Michael, isn't the stuff you're writing about

00:01:08.469 --> 00:01:09.869
addictive like drugs?

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:12.189
And if so, how can we have any control over?

00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:14.479
And I was like, whoa, what do you mean?

00:01:14.479 --> 00:01:16.090
You're comparing like Twinkies to heroin?

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:18.780
I mean, I was like, no way.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:26.069
But I came full circle in realizing and talking
to experts who coached me on this, which is

00:01:26.069 --> 00:01:31.829
that, look, in some ways food is even more
addictive than some drugs.

00:01:31.829 --> 00:01:38.140
And part of the reason for that is that the
companies have figured out ways to tap into

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.959
exploit, if you will, our basic instincts.

00:01:41.959 --> 00:01:47.240
I mean, we by nature are drawn not just to
eat, but to overeat.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:51.969
And the companies have figured that out and
turned that, which was a good thing for most

00:01:51.969 --> 00:01:55.609
of our existence into a really treacherous
thing.

00:01:55.609 --> 00:02:04.270
By making over eating an everyday thing is
what is the start of the chain of all of this?

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:06.399
Is it profit motive for food giants?

00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:11.970
You know, I mean, look, I still don't see
this as an evil empire that intentionally

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:15.070
set out to to make us overweight or otherwise.

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:19.770
These are companies doing what all companies
want to do, make as much money as possible

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:26.300
by selling this product as possible and by
making that product as attractive and irresistible

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.060
as as possible.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:33.390
So they have chemists working for them who
have marketing experts, psychologists, sort

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:40.680
of all trained and focused on hitting our
biological instincts, but also our emotional

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.550
buttons to maximize the allure of their products.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:49.820
Can you talk a little bit about what what
exactly is it when you researched this, that

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:51.500
that is most addictive?

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:56.970
Is it is sometimes said, you know, the dopamine
release from sugar is similar to drugs.

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:58.470
Is it what exactly is it?

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.490
Yeah, so there's a few things.

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:03.550
One, we are built to sort of love food that's
inexpensive.

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:05.430
It kind of makes sense, right?

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:09.180
The least put out the least amount of energy
for the calories that you're getting.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:14.030
You know, the industry uses these chemical
labs to come up with new formulations that

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:20.570
are shaving, you know, 10 cents, 20 cents
off the price of a package that excite the

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:21.710
brain.

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:24.160
They're really good at variety.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:29.080
You know, we're we, by instinct are drawn
toward different kinds of food.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:33.050
That's why we're able to populate different
parts of the world with different food to

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:34.050
eat.

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:35.450
And we're really good at adjusting to that.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:41.210
You know, you walk in the cereal aisle of
the grocery store, there are two hundred versions

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:45.110
of sugar is charged with that sole purpose
of exciting the brand.

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:46.590
We love calories, right.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:54.160
We have sensors in our gut, also possibly
in our mouth that detect calories in food.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:59.990
And so the food companies have been really
good about packing in the calories.

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:07.040
I mean, compare like a hot pocket to a stick
of celery and you get the idea of the contrast

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:11.060
between basic foodstuffs and ultra processed
foods.

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:16.739
And again, the brain the brain gets really
excited by the more calories and is much more

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:21.340
apt to sort of put you into that impulsive
compulsive drive.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:25.710
We're the thinking part of your brain doesn't
have a chance to step up and say, wait a minute,

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:29.440
Michael, I think you're going too hard on
this food.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:34.380
It seems that at a certain point there was
a huge evolutionary evolutionary advantage

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:39.770
to that in the sense that, like, if you think
back to Hunter gathering and the amount of

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:45.970
time and energy spent chewing in order to
obtain a certain number of calories, and then

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:51.000
all of a sudden you may be able to, you know,
you cook something and you're so which allows

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:56.620
you to kind of do in advance some of the work
that your teeth and body would do.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:02.120
The body probably would sense that, wow, one
bite of this is conferring way more calories.

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:03.910
And it was good at a certain point.

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:09.570
It was such an eye opener to me, spending
time with evolutionary biologists, one of

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:13.400
whom kind of pointed out to me, look, Michael,
it's not so much that food is addictive.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:19.680
It's that we by nature are drawn to eating
and the companies have changed the nature

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.210
of our food.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:26.460
You know, you said it absolutely body fat
was a really good thing.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:30.070
It enabled our brains to grow, enable us to
get through hard time.

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:36.040
They've allowed us to have more babies, which
are natural selection is all about.

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:41.880
And it's only in the last 50 years that we're
sort of realizing that having too much body

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:43.260
fat will work against you.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.320
I have no idea.

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:49.060
But body fat is a living thinking organ.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:51.530
And when you try to reduce.

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:56.570
About on your body, it will fight back an
incredibly diabolical ways, it will send signals

00:05:56.570 --> 00:06:02.080
to the brain that you're hungry when you're
not hungry, it will slow down your metabolism.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:09.500
It will make you get more excited when you
see advertising for processed food and cause

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:15.430
you to want that food more than you did before
you had that extra weight.

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:21.840
One of the things that Michael Pollan writes
about in Omnivore's Dilemma is the sort of

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:27.961
transformation by food companies of foods
into food products, taking, for example, the

00:06:27.961 --> 00:06:34.370
idea of like if you just take corn, it's tough
to make a lot more money just selling corn.

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:38.950
Maybe you can get a seed that can be planted
more densely so you can sort of like up how

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:40.910
much you can grow in an acre.

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:44.300
But like selling corn as corn is only going
to get you so far.

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:48.200
However, if you take corn, break it down into
its component parts.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:52.580
You can now use corn sirup as a as an alternative
to sugar.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:56.760
You're adding calories and reducing cost and
you're extracting and taking all these different

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:57.960
elements.

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:02.860
Most people, if they looked at a list of ingredients,
would recognize a lot of that stuff is highly

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:05.360
processed and probably not that good for you.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:09.139
And yet these products are all incredibly
popular.

00:07:09.139 --> 00:07:10.139
So.

00:07:10.139 --> 00:07:13.830
So what are food companies doing in order
to make people want products that if you're

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:17.580
really presented with the ingredient list,
most people probably know aren't really the

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:18.919
best things to be eating?

00:07:18.919 --> 00:07:23.260
Yeah, I think one of the most important things
they're doing is speed.

00:07:23.260 --> 00:07:28.610
I learned from researchers who study drug
addiction, whether it's cigarets, alcohol

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:34.991
or heart or narcotics, that the hallmark of
addiction is how fast the substance hits the

00:07:34.991 --> 00:07:40.930
brain, because the faster it gets in our brain,
the more apt we are to hit that go button

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:45.200
and not respond to the smaller part of the
brain that's trying to put the brakes on,

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:49.310
you know, us doing something we really don't
want to be doing.

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:56.720
And so the food companies were ingenious in
building speed into their product, both in

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:00.810
the factory to reduce the cost, but also just
the way we eat it.

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:05.280
And I often refer to these foods as convenience
foods because a lot of them could be eaten

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:09.090
with one hand while you're doing something
else.

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:15.940
And that creates this mindless this mode where
you're not paying attention to the food and

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:20.940
when you're not paying attention to their
products, they're controlling you rather than

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:22.040
you controlling them.

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:28.580
The speed is speed is probably the most important
and most diabolical aspect of these ultra

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:29.710
processed foods.

00:08:29.710 --> 00:08:36.539
How does all of this apply to the sort of
diets sometimes sometimes called fad diets,

00:08:36.539 --> 00:08:42.430
lifestyle diets that sometimes will come in
and out of favor in large part because of

00:08:42.430 --> 00:08:47.890
marketing, when it seems that over time, if
you take a big picture view, you don't really

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:54.130
seem to need anything so specialized that
seems that sort of limiting processed foods

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:55.290
is a good thing.

00:08:55.290 --> 00:09:03.339
Generally limiting, but not necessarily completely
eliminating animal products isn't a bad thing

00:09:03.339 --> 00:09:04.339
to me.

00:09:04.339 --> 00:09:06.200
I see profit opportunities.

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:13.260
Any time you see a diet that requires a lot
of one thing or none of another is my my instinct

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:14.260
right on that.

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:15.290
Well, absolutely.

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:18.950
And let me tell you about sort of profit.

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:26.910
I was stunned to learn that the largest processed
food manufacturers quietly bought up gain

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:31.440
control over some of our more popular dieting
methods.

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:40.860
And we're talking about Hynde's buying Weight
Watchers, Slim Fast, Atkins, you name it,

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:42.149
South Beach Diet.

00:09:42.149 --> 00:09:47.510
A lot of these brands and methods were acquired
none other than by the food companies.

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:53.910
And then they kind of populated the grocery
store with diet versions of their mainline

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:59.380
products, which really weren't all that different
then to full bore calorie version, sitting

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:04.860
next to each other on the shelf and sort of
counting on us to be making rational decisions

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:12.830
every time we go shopping as to which of these
products to to to to buy and eat and pay attention

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:13.830
to.

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:18.570
But here's the thing again, that sort of relates
to that relates to addiction.

00:10:18.570 --> 00:10:22.220
The thing about that and one of the lessons
from drug addiction, one of the things that

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:27.560
makes these food products even more troublesome
than drugs can be, which is that you can't

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:28.700
just go cold turkey.

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:33.830
You can't just stop eating to deal with a
food eating problem.

00:10:33.830 --> 00:10:36.149
We all have to eat.

00:10:36.149 --> 00:10:37.690
And that's the slippery slope.

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:43.279
And so we walk into the grocery store and
we're so vulnerable at times in our lives

00:10:43.279 --> 00:10:50.570
or times of the day to their marketing and
their products and these diet schemes that

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:51.570
look.

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:57.589
By and large, work until they stop working,
which is why so many people have trouble sticking

00:10:57.589 --> 00:11:04.490
to a diet, because it's strange and it's different,
and ultimately because the power of memory

00:11:04.490 --> 00:11:11.830
and is keeping our eating habits draws us
back to what we were doing before is the most

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:15.390
people most people are busy, people are working,
people are earning a living.

00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:16.779
They have to get their kids back and forth.

00:11:16.779 --> 00:11:19.100
It can be very difficult to keep up with.

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:21.320
Is the egg good or bad right now?

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:22.730
Is coffee good or bad?

00:11:22.730 --> 00:11:24.459
Is soy good about that?

00:11:24.459 --> 00:11:30.410
Is there one principle which is just avoid
processed foods as much as possible?

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:33.620
That is the simplest thing for people to remember.

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:35.810
Or would it be something else?

00:11:35.810 --> 00:11:37.930
I think it kind of depends where you are on
the spectrum.

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:40.670
Look, I wrote this book for everybody.

00:11:40.670 --> 00:11:45.910
Whether you're in an eating disorder with
bingeing or you've got clinical obesity or

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:49.690
you're at the other end where you're just
kind of like troubled by, like, how much attention

00:11:49.690 --> 00:11:54.830
you have to pay to food to stay on top of
it and not let it like, you know, gain control

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:56.080
of your of your life.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:01.980
And I think the overall lesson I think, again,
a lesson learned from the drug addiction world

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:08.980
is that it really helps to have a plan no
matter what it is, but to have that plan and

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:10.670
then execute it.

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:17.280
So, you know, if you're hit by the proverbial
3:00 p.m. craving for a cookie, what you learn

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:23.370
from people who've helped people deal with
drug addiction is you've got to like it, 255.

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:28.670
You need to be working on your your plan,
whether that involves getting up and stretching

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:34.540
or calling a friend or having an alternative
snack like a handful of nuts in order to ward

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:41.360
off that craving grief, because those cravings
come on so fast that no amount of willpower

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:47.149
is going to help you resist them once they
hit the reward center of the brain so that

00:12:47.149 --> 00:12:52.399
that priest thought, no matter what kind of
what your strategy is, I think is the overall

00:12:52.399 --> 00:12:57.820
lesson I learned in talking to the other two
addiction researchers in terms of now all

00:12:57.820 --> 00:13:04.500
of the sugar alternatives, you know, sweetened
with beet sugar, sweetened with Monck fruit

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:12.140
or whatever the case may be, it is that really
about health or is it about profitability

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:15.060
or is it both?

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:18.770
The companies are in this mode now where more
and more of us are caring about what we put

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:24.860
in our bodies and responding to that by eating
less of their most problematic products, the

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:31.430
companies are responding by cutting back on
their fat, but also adding things to their

00:13:31.430 --> 00:13:37.580
products, like protein or fiber, you know,
on the notion that that might help reduce

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:39.829
the cravings we get for those products.

00:13:39.829 --> 00:13:42.480
Sugar is really interesting to me.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:46.290
And I'm, you know, and the science is still
new on this.

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:49.970
And I wouldn't know I wouldn't bet on it at
this point.

00:13:49.970 --> 00:13:57.120
But there is concern that this move by the
industry to replace reduced sugar by replacing

00:13:57.120 --> 00:14:03.149
it with fake non-caloric sweeteners could
be really problematic.

00:14:03.149 --> 00:14:04.589
I mean, think about it.

00:14:04.589 --> 00:14:06.660
You know, you taste something sweet.

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:11.670
Your brain is expecting calories, but your
gut doesn't get those calories.

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:17.120
You know, it's quite possible that something
will go haywire with your metabolism.

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:24.200
So, I mean, if you're using a diet soda to
